WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.550
PRIYANKA: If you are
ingesting large amount of data

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.860
for analysis or
looking to simplify

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.620
the development of event
driven microservices,

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.295
then you need to check
out Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:00:10.295 --> 00:00:12.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.430
Welcome to Pub/Sub Made Easy.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.260
I am Priyanka.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.540
And in today's episode, we will
dive deeper into Publishers

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.200
and see some configuration
and application design choices

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.020
you will need to make when
building a real world Publisher

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.730
application.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.680
A Publisher application
creates and sends messages

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.796
to a desired topic
on Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:00:36.796 --> 00:00:39.890
Cloud Pub/Sub offers at
least once message delivery

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.040
and best effort ordering
to existing subscribers.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.410
While publishing
messages to a topic

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.200
when using JSON over
REST, the message data

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.360
must be base64 encoded.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.860
The entire request, including
one or many messages,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:57.260
must be smaller than
10 MB after decoding.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.450
The message payload
must not be empty.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.990
It must contain either a
nonempty data field or at least

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:04.760
one attribute.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.370
What can you include
in the message itself?

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.450
Well, here's the message schema.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.250
You can embed custom attributes
as metadata in the messages.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.280
Attributes can be text
strings or byte strings.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.490
A server generated ID
unique within the topic

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.000
is returned on the successful
publication of a message.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.160
If you would like to see
an example of publishing

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.310
a message, check out
the previous episode.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.570
All client libraries
offer an asynchronous

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.700
API where your
application does not

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.920
have to wait for the
actual published request

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.130
to be complete, although
synchronous or blocking

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.058
mode of operation is
supported as well.

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:42.350
Check out the link in the
description below for the API

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.720
reference documentation for
your choice of programming

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.610
language and its support.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.890
The first decision
you have to make

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.870
is how to batch your messages.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.580
You can configure
the client library

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.860
to send multiple
messages per request

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.470
to the service based on
the size of the buffer

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.310
or time the client waits
for the buffer to fill.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.410
Batching has the
obvious advantage.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.600
The larger batch size
increases message throughput,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.750
which is the rate of
messages sent per CPU.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.780
Asynchronous client
libraries try

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.070
to publish multiple batches in
parallel for higher throughput

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.240
but it also comes at a cost.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.020
It introduces latency
for individual messages,

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.590
which are cued in memory until
their corresponding batch is

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.560
filled and ready to be
sent over the network.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.900
To minimize latency, batching
should be turned off.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.080
This is particularly
important for applications

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.110
that publish a single message
as part of the request response

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.290
sequence.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.460
However, the additional
network and computing overhead

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.590
may make small
batches preferable.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:44.690
You'll have to tune your system.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.260
Messages can be batched based
on request size in bytes,

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.510
number of messages, and time.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.420
You can override the default
settings by defining your own.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.223
Here's an example
code in Python where

00:02:56.223 --> 00:02:59.840
we are defining batch settings
for maximum bytes, latency,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.100
and messages.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.740
A caveat here is that once
you override one setting,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.060
you should override
them all explicitly.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.620
Now here's how this works.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.050
On the first message
that you send,

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:15.800
a new batch is automatically
created with a countdown.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.100
For every subsequent
message, if there is already

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.070
a valid batch that is
still accepting messages,

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:25.400
then that batch is used
to drop the new message.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.400
The batch is published once
sufficient time has elapsed,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.150
maximum messages has reached,
or maximum bytes is met.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:36.510
By default, the batch
setting is set 0.05 seconds.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.050
Now what happens if
publishing fails?

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.030
Publishing failures
are automatically

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.250
retried in some
client libraries.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:44.780
Check out the
linked documentation

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.810
to check the support for
the language of your choice.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.840
Because we are dealing with
requests over a network,

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.010
request failure is a normal
part of life and understanding

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.060
retries is essential.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.190
Client libraries support
custom retry settings.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.610
Here's a snippet
with retry error code

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.230
received from the publisher.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:05.390
Here's a snippet with
retried parameters

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.050
that include timeouts and delays
with the default settings.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:10.790
The main two parameters
to understand

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:14.330
are the total timeout, which is
how long the application will

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:18.180
keep retrying before giving
up, and the initial timeout,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.170
which is how soon it
will start retrying.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.810
The total time out depends
on the typical availability

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.690
of your network, the memory
available to your machines,

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.380
and application
specific considerations.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:29.880
All right.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.270
So today we learned
how to publish messages

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.640
in Cloud Pub/Sub,
different options including

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.470
synchronous/asynchronous
messages,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.960
and setting up batches
and custom attributes.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.200
Join us next time to learn more
about setting up Subscriber

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.790
to receive messages.

